WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:03.067
-Welcome back to "Late Night."
We're here with Billy Porter.

00:00:03.067 --> 00:00:04.667
So this is a romantic comedy

00:00:04.667 --> 00:00:06.968
that focuses around
a trans character.

00:00:06.968 --> 00:00:09.400
Was it rewarding to tell
this kind of story

00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:11.868
that we rarely, if ever, see?

00:00:11.868 --> 00:00:14.100
-Yeah, we need to put
some joy out into the world.

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:15.801
-I think that's right.
-And, you know, coming --

00:00:15.801 --> 00:00:17.934
[ Cheers and applause ]
Yeah, and coming from "Pose,"

00:00:17.934 --> 00:00:23.834
most of the trans stories and
LGBTQ+ stories that we see,

00:00:23.834 --> 00:00:26.601
particularly trans,
are always about the trauma.

00:00:26.601 --> 00:00:27.734
-Mm-hmm.

00:00:27.734 --> 00:00:30.734
-And I was so drawn to
this piece because

00:00:30.734 --> 00:00:32.200
it's about trans joy.

00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:33.901
It's about celebration.

00:00:33.901 --> 00:00:37.567
It's about the normalizing
and the humanizing

00:00:37.567 --> 00:00:40.968
of a group of people that have
been dehumanized.

00:00:40.968 --> 00:00:46.868
And so it's in the spirit
of old John Hughes movies,

00:00:46.868 --> 00:00:49.467
coming-of-age rom-coms,

00:00:49.467 --> 00:00:52.567
and the characters look like
what the world looks like today.

00:00:52.567 --> 00:00:55.167
And I just feel so blessed to
be able to sort of

00:00:55.167 --> 00:00:58.567
usher this kind of art out
into the world.

00:00:58.567 --> 00:01:00.234
It's really lovely.

00:01:00.234 --> 00:01:01.367
-That is really special.

00:01:01.367 --> 00:01:04.667
It's also, as you mentioned,
you did it.

00:01:04.667 --> 00:01:06.167
You went back,
you filmed it in Pittsburgh.

00:01:06.167 --> 00:01:07.968
-Yes.
-And you got to pull people

00:01:07.968 --> 00:01:10.167
from your past life into
the whole production.

00:01:10.167 --> 00:01:13.734
-Yeah, you know,
I was reading the script

00:01:13.734 --> 00:01:15.868
and it just happened to be
written in Pittsburgh,

00:01:15.868 --> 00:01:18.400
and when I was casting,
I thought,

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:22.000
"Oh, I have so many angels
there, so many friends there,"

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:25.868
so most of the characters that
are ancillary

00:01:25.868 --> 00:01:29.567
are either my friends,
like the mother of Khal

00:01:29.567 --> 00:01:33.834
and the nurse and the teacher,

00:01:33.834 --> 00:01:36.734
we all did Kennywood Park
together back in the '80s.

00:01:36.734 --> 00:01:38.234
-Alright, so real quick,
Kennywood Park

00:01:38.234 --> 00:01:39.968
is the Pittsburgh
amusement park.

00:01:39.968 --> 00:01:41.167
-Yes.
-You worked there?

00:01:41.167 --> 00:01:43.467
-I worked there for three
summers, doing the shows.

00:01:43.467 --> 00:01:45.868
-What were the shows?
-Oh, my God.

00:01:45.868 --> 00:01:48.968
I can't even remember.
They were good, though.

00:01:48.968 --> 00:01:50.267
Six different shows.

00:01:50.267 --> 00:01:51.968
-So you weren't, like,
selling funnel cake

00:01:51.968 --> 00:01:54.400
or tickets to the Tilt-a-Whirl.
-No, no.

00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:57.067
I was on the stage, letting
the kids have it, honey.

00:01:57.067 --> 00:01:58.834
You know, singing --

00:01:58.834 --> 00:02:01.667
♪ Wake me up before you go-go ♪

00:02:01.667 --> 00:02:03.601
You know? We were performing.

00:02:03.601 --> 00:02:05.734
So I had those people.

00:02:05.734 --> 00:02:09.634
One of my mentors that got me
to Carnegie Mellon,

00:02:09.634 --> 00:02:17.234
Lenora Nemetz, a Fosse dancer,
she plays the art teacher.

00:02:17.234 --> 00:02:19.367
Vanita Harbour
and Renee Goldsberry,

00:02:19.367 --> 00:02:21.567
we all went to Carnegie Mellon
together,

00:02:21.567 --> 00:02:24.067
so they play the two moms.

00:02:24.067 --> 00:02:28.033
Billy Hartung, who I went to
dance class with

00:02:28.033 --> 00:02:29.801
on the other side of town.

00:02:29.801 --> 00:02:34.300
His mom and dad would put us
in the pickup truck

00:02:34.300 --> 00:02:36.868
and drive me all the way to
the other side of town.

00:02:36.868 --> 00:02:39.901
Home at 10:00 after dance class.
You know?

00:02:39.901 --> 00:02:43.868
It's really a love letter
to Pittsburgh

00:02:43.868 --> 00:02:48.534
and the chosen family and
the mentors and the friends

00:02:48.534 --> 00:02:50.567
who raised me, because
I wouldn't be here

00:02:50.567 --> 00:02:51.901
if it wasn't for that.

00:02:51.901 --> 00:02:55.200
And a lot of people still think
that Pittsburgh is like,

00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:56.901
this old, depressed steel town.

00:02:56.901 --> 00:02:59.267
And child, it's not.

00:02:59.267 --> 00:03:00.400
[ Applause ]

00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:02.501
She's fabulous
and she's progressive.

00:03:02.501 --> 00:03:04.400
-Yeah?
-And she's, you know,

00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:07.367
Obama had the first
G20 summit there,

00:03:07.367 --> 00:03:10.968
because Pittsburgh was one of
the only cities

00:03:10.968 --> 00:03:14.400
that was not affected by
the 2008 recession.

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:17.634
So I wanted to, like,
give Pittsburgh some shine.

00:03:17.634 --> 00:03:20.067
-Yeah, well, you know that will
enamor you to my father,

00:03:20.067 --> 00:03:21.801
who's a child of Pittsburgh.
-I know!

00:03:21.801 --> 00:03:23.234
-His favorite guests are

00:03:23.234 --> 00:03:24.868
the ones who talk kindly
about Pittsburgh.

00:03:24.868 --> 00:03:27.901
A lot of times, directors,
I feel like

00:03:27.901 --> 00:03:30.100
maybe the stereotype is
they look a little

00:03:30.100 --> 00:03:32.167
schlubby on set, you know?

00:03:32.167 --> 00:03:33.467
They obviously are very busy.

00:03:33.467 --> 00:03:35.100
They don't have time to worry
about their outfits.

00:03:35.100 --> 00:03:36.267
I'm happy to report that was

00:03:36.267 --> 00:03:38.434
not the case with
director Billy Porter.

00:03:38.434 --> 00:03:39.434
Look at you!

00:03:39.434 --> 00:03:42.000
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:45.667
You just don't take a day off,
do you?

00:03:45.667 --> 00:03:47.834
[ Applause ]

00:03:47.834 --> 00:03:49.834
-You know,
that's a caftan moment.

00:03:49.834 --> 00:03:53.200
I found the caftan situation
during COVID.

00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:56.734
It's a really, really easy
silhouette.

00:03:56.734 --> 00:03:58.834
-Yeah.
-You get a good pattern,

00:03:58.834 --> 00:04:01.601
and you just put it on,
and I'm like Bea Arthur.

00:04:01.601 --> 00:04:03.067
-Yeah.
-You know?

00:04:03.067 --> 00:04:05.501
-We have to find things from
the pandemic

00:04:05.501 --> 00:04:07.234
that were teachable moments.

00:04:07.234 --> 00:04:10.467
-Yes, and so I had
those moments,

00:04:10.467 --> 00:04:12.667
and, you know, like I said
the last time,

00:04:12.667 --> 00:04:13.868
I want to be
the Head Bitch in Charge,

00:04:13.868 --> 00:04:15.734
so you gotta dress for
the job you want.

00:04:15.734 --> 00:04:18.801
The one you have, honey,
every day.

00:04:18.801 --> 00:04:20.934
[ Applause ]
-Wait, my last question.

00:04:20.934 --> 00:04:23.267
And by the way, I do like
checking in on

00:04:23.267 --> 00:04:25.267
the progress of you achieving
Head Bitch in Charge.

00:04:25.267 --> 00:04:26.601
-Yes, yes.
-I do feel like every time,

00:04:26.601 --> 00:04:28.901
we're a little closer.
-I'm closer and closer.

00:04:28.901 --> 00:04:30.200
-One might say you've
achieved it,

00:04:30.200 --> 00:04:31.868
but we don't want to put
your fire out.

00:04:31.868 --> 00:04:34.801
-No, not yet.
I have some more [bleep] to do.

00:04:34.801 --> 00:04:37.100
I only said that because I think
I just heard you

00:04:37.100 --> 00:04:40.300
drop the [bleep] bomb earlier.

00:04:40.300 --> 00:04:42.367
We're allowed to do that now?

00:04:42.367 --> 00:04:43.868
-Yeah!
-Alright.

00:04:43.868 --> 00:04:47.934
-We're on very late at night.
-Get ready, bitches!

00:04:47.934 --> 00:04:49.868
I work blue.

00:04:49.868 --> 00:04:52.968
[ Laughter ]

00:04:52.968 --> 00:04:54.834
Ooh, I made you laugh.
-You did make me laugh.

00:04:54.834 --> 00:04:57.734
It's fun to have somebody
say they work blue

00:04:57.734 --> 00:04:59.634
when there's so much blue.

00:04:59.634 --> 00:05:02.534
There's so much actual blue,
on the eyes and...

00:05:02.534 --> 00:05:03.601
Alright, ready?

00:05:03.601 --> 00:05:05.267
This director's chair. Explain.

00:05:05.267 --> 00:05:07.701
-I don't like director's chairs.

00:05:07.701 --> 00:05:10.934
They are awkward.
They are uncomfortable.

00:05:10.934 --> 00:05:11.934
-Uh-huh.

00:05:11.934 --> 00:05:15.667
-And I don't like
sitting in them.

00:05:15.667 --> 00:05:21.367
And so, you know, Ryan Murphy,
my mentor,

00:05:21.367 --> 00:05:25.400
comes to work to direct
with his own chair.

00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:27.467
They bring a chair in for him,
and I was like,

00:05:27.467 --> 00:05:30.467
"I'mma do my Ryan Murphy thing,
get my own chair!"

00:05:30.467 --> 00:05:32.667
-Well, you deserve it.
-And so I got my own chair,

00:05:32.667 --> 00:05:34.100
and it was comfortable.

00:05:34.100 --> 00:05:37.934
-There you go. Caftan, chair,
movie, check, check, check.

00:05:37.934 --> 00:05:39.234
Thank you for being here, Billy.

00:05:39.234 --> 00:05:40.400
-Thank you.
-Always such a pleasure.

00:05:40.400 --> 00:05:41.601
Love having you.
-Thank you, thank you.

00:05:41.601 --> 00:05:42.901
-Billy Porter,

00:05:42.901 --> 00:05:45.901
"Anything's Possible" is
streaming now on Prime Video.

00:05:45.901 --> 00:05:48.300
We'll be right back
with Jane Mayer, everybody.

